      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Hendersonville''' is a city located in [[Sumner County, Tennessee]], on Old Hickory Lake.  As of the [[2000]] census, the city had a total population of 40,620.

== Geography ==
[[Image:TNMap-doton-Hendersonville.PNG|right|Location of Hendersonville, Tennessee]]
Hendersonville is located at 36&amp;deg;18'0&quot; North, 86&amp;deg;36'22&quot; West (36.300084, -86.606109){{GR|1}}.

According to the [[United States Census Bureau]], the city has a total area of 85.2 [[square kilometer|km&amp;sup2;]] (32.9 [[square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]]).  70.8 km&amp;sup2; (27.3 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and 14.4 km&amp;sup2; (5.6 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is water.  The total area is 16.93% water.

== Demographics ==
As of the [[census]]{{GR|2}} of [[2000]], there are 40,620 people, 15,823 households, and 11,566 families residing in the city.  The [[population density]] is 573.9/km&amp;sup2; (1,486.4/mi&amp;sup2;).  There are 16,507 housing units at an average density of 233.2/km&amp;sup2; (604.0/mi&amp;sup2;).  The racial makeup of the city is 92.93% [[White (U.S. Census)|White]], 4.12% [[African American (U.S. Census)|African American]], 0.27% [[Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]], 1.10% [[Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]], 0.03% [[Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander]], 0.65% from [[Race (U.S. Census)|other races]], and 0.90% from two or more races.  1.71% of the population are [[Hispanic (U.S. Census)|Hispanic]] or [[Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 15,823 households out of which 35.7% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 59.3% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 10.7% have a female householder with no husband present, and 26.9% are non-families. 22.3% of all households are made up of individuals and 6.5% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older.  The average household size is 2.55 and the average family size is 3.00.

In the city the population is spread out with 25.8% under the age of 18, 7.8% from 18 to 24, 31.5% from 25 to 44, 24.8% from 45 to 64, and 10.2% who are 65 years of age or older.  The median age is 36 years.  For every 100 females there are 94.9 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 90.3 males.

The median income for a household in the city is $50,108, and the median income for a family is $57,625. Males have a median income of $40,823 versus $27,771 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the city is $24,165.  6.2% of the population and 5.2% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 8.2% of those under the age of 18 and 7.7% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

== History ==

Hendersonville was settled around [[1784]] by Daniel Smith when he began work on [[Historic Rock Castle|Rock Castle]].  In [[1790]], [[William Henderson]], for whom the area was named, settled in.  With the completion of [[Old Hickory Dam]] in [[1954]], the city of Hendersonville started to grow into the most populous city of [[Sumner County, Tennessee|Sumner County]] and also one of the most populous suburbs of Nashville, along with [[Smyrna, Tennessee|Smyrna]] and [[Brentwood, Tennessee|Brentwood]].  The city now contains around 0.7% of the population of Tennessee.

== Famous Hendersonvillians ==

*[[Johnny Cash]], country singer
*[[June Carter Cash]], country singer
*Daniel Smith, early pioneer
*[[Conway Twitty]], country singer
*James Wilhoit, [[University of Tennessee|UT Vols]] place kicker
*[[Roy Orbison]], rock-n-roll singer
*[[Marty Stuart]], country singer
*[[Connie Smith]], country singer
*[[Jeff Jarrett]], [[professional wrestler]]
*Mitch Huffman


{{Tennessee}}

