      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Burke''' is a city located in southwestern [[Angelina County, Texas]].  As of the [[2000]] census, the city had a total population of 315.

== History ==

Burke was founded in 1881 when construction of the Houston, East and West Texas Railway reached the western edge of Bradley Prairie ([[Bradley Prairie, Texas]]). The town was originally named Rhodes for general store owner W. R. Rhodes and postmaster H. R. Rhodes, but about 1885 it was renamed for Edmund L. Burke, a railroad employee who directed the railroad survey. 

By 1885 Burke had three sawmills, three cotton gins, a church, and a school. In 1886 it received a post office, which remained there until 1955. By 1888 it had a larger school, three general stores, a drugstore, a sawmill, a dentist and watchmaker, and a Farmers' Alliance store. In 1897 Burke had an estimated population of 650. By 1904 Burke's population had declined to 161, due to the rise of [[Lufkin, Texas]], eight miles north, as an industrial center for the county. However, by 1915 the population had risen to 200, and by 1925 it reached 300. The Burke Methodist Church was organized in 1899-1900, and the First Baptist Church of Burke in 1905.

Education was a priority at Burke, and it had an independent school district until 1964 when the Burke school was consolidated with the Diboll Independent School District at [[Diboll, Texas]]. Burke was incorported as a city in 1966, and a municipal water system was constructed. Burke had a population of 322 in 1980 and 314 in 1990.

== Geography ==
[[Image:TXMap-doton-Burke.PNG|right|Location of Burke, Texas]]
Burke is located at 31&amp;deg;13'43&quot; North, 94&amp;deg;46'10&quot; West (31.228501, -94.769449){{GR|1}}.  survey.

According to the [[United States Census Bureau]], the city has a total area of 1.6 [[square kilometer|km&amp;sup2;]] (0.6 [[square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]]).  1.6 km&amp;sup2; (0.6 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and none of the area is covered with water.

== Demographics ==
As of the [[census]]{{GR|2}} of [[2000]], there are 315 people, 114 households, and 85 families residing in the city.  The [[population density]] is 193.1/km&amp;sup2; (501.5/mi&amp;sup2;).  There are 138 housing units at an average density of 84.6/km&amp;sup2; (219.7/mi&amp;sup2;).  The racial makeup of the city is 91.43% [[White (U.S. Census)|White]], 1.27% [[African American (U.S. Census)|African American]], 1.90% [[Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]], 0.00% [[Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]], 0.00% [[Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander]], 3.81% from [[Race (U.S. Census)|other races]], and 1.59% from two or more races.  22.22% of the population are [[Hispanic (U.S. Census)|Hispanic]] or [[Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 114 households out of which 36.8% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 59.6% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 10.5% have a female householder with no husband present, and 25.4% are non-families. 23.7% of all households are made up of individuals and 12.3% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older.  The average household size is 2.76 and the average family size is 3.28.

In the city the population is spread out with 27.0% under the age of 18, 12.4% from 18 to 24, 30.2% from 25 to 44, 16.2% from 45 to 64, and 14.3% who are 65 years of age or older.  The median age is 31 years.  For every 100 females there are 86.4 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 88.5 males.

The median income for a household in the city is $29,821, and the median income for a family is $34,375. Males have a median income of $31,250 versus $18,472 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the city is $12,033.  14.4% of the population and 15.7% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 9.9% of those under the age of 18 and 17.4% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

== External links ==
