      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Archer City''' is a city located in [[Archer County, Texas]].  As of the [[2000]] census, the city had a total population of 1,848.  It is the [[county seat]] of [[Archer County, Texas|Archer County]]{{GR|6}}. The town is named for [[Branch Tanner Archer]], a commissioner for the [[Republic of Texas]]. Its most famous native son is the author [[Larry McMurtry]].

== Geography ==
[[Image:TXMap-doton-ArcherCity.PNG|right|Location of Archer City, Texas]]
Archer City is located at 33&amp;deg;35'39&quot; North, 98&amp;deg;37'35&quot; West (33.594036, -98.626499){{GR|1}}.

According to the [[United States Census Bureau]], the city has a total area of 5.7 [[square kilometer|km&amp;sup2;]] (2.2 [[square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]]).  5.7 km&amp;sup2; (2.2 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and 0.1 km&amp;sup2; (0.04 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is water.  The total area is 0.90% water.

== Demographics ==
As of the [[census]]{{GR|2}} of [[2000]], there are 1,848 people, 758 households, and 506 families residing in the city.  The [[population density]] is 322.9/km&amp;sup2; (837.3/mi&amp;sup2;).  There are 869 housing units at an average density of 151.8/km&amp;sup2; (393.7/mi&amp;sup2;).  The racial makeup of the city is 97.56% [[White (U.S. Census)|White]], 0.00% [[African American (U.S. Census)|African American]], 0.70% [[Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]], 0.00% [[Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]], 0.00% [[Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander]], 0.81% from [[Race (U.S. Census)|other races]], and 0.92% from two or more races.  2.33% of the population are [[Hispanic (U.S. Census)|Hispanic]] or [[Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 758 households out of which 33.5% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 56.2% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 8.4% have a female householder with no husband present, and 33.2% are non-families. 30.5% of all households are made up of individuals and 14.6% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older.  The average household size is 2.38 and the average family size is 2.99.

In the city the population is spread out with 25.6% under the age of 18, 7.5% from 18 to 24, 25.9% from 25 to 44, 22.5% from 45 to 64, and 18.5% who are 65 years of age or older.  The median age is 39 years.  For every 100 females there are 88.6 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 83.1 males.

The median income for a household in the city is $29,886, and the median income for a family is $36,563. Males have a median income of $29,524 versus $18,977 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the city is $19,140.  13.5% of the population and 11.3% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 13.8% of those under the age of 18 and 17.9% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

== Education ==
There is one school campus at Archer City.  It encompasses the High School, Junior High, and Elementary grades.  Grades 7-12 are all in the same building.  

== Athletic Department ==
The Athletic Director and Head Football coach is none other than [[Steve Smith]].  Since his arrival in 2004, the football team has gone a combined 5-15.  The basketball team has fared slightly better under the direction of [[Casey Rodgers]].  The bright spot of the athletic department, however, has been baseball, which is coached by [[Pat Maxwell]], a former major league baseball player for the Cleveland Indians.  In 2005, the team made it to the Regional Semi-Finals for the second year in a row, under the senior leadership of [[Mark Wuthrich]] and [[Matt Ridenour]].  The All-District players were pretty much the entire team.  All-State included [[Ty Robertson]] / P (3rd Team), [[Caleb Truette]] / OF (2nd Team), Mark Wuthrich / SS (Honorable Mention), [[Andrew Swinhoe]] / 3rd Base (Honorable Mention), and [[Matthew Sherrill]] / OF (Honorable Mention).

== External links ==
[http://www.esc9.net/acisd/ Archer City Independent School District Official Website]

{{Mapit-US-cityscale|33.594036|-98.626499}}

